httpd — minimal HTTP/1.0 echo server
=====================================

Phase-5-ish milestone: prove the slix TCP stack coexists with a
real-world host-side HTTP client. Listens on port 8080, accepts one
client, drains the request headers until `\r\n\r\n`, replies with a
canned `HTTP/1.0 200 OK` + `hello\n`, closes, exits.

Single-shot by design — multi-client concurrency is the follow-up
chunk. The test harness starts us via `httpd` (foreground), waits
for the `listening` log line, dials through QEMU's
`hostfwd=tcp::28083-:8080` into the guest, reads until close, and
asserts the response shape. We exit after one served request so
the shell gets its prompt back.

Out of scope:

- HTTP/1.1 keep-alive, HEAD, chunked bodies, routing, 404s.
- Multiple concurrent accepts (needs the `test_tcp_mcl` fix first).

    module oskit.bin.httpd

    import oskit.ulib.{puts, exit, exit_with}
    import oskit.net.{tcp_listen, tcp_accept, tcp_send, tcp_recv, tcp_close}


Decimal helper — used only for diagnostic log lines.

    print_udec(v: int)
        if v >= 10
            print_udec(v / 10)
        var buf: [1]byte
        buf[0] = byte('0' + (v % 10))
        puts(string(&buf[0], 1))


Scan `req[0..used)` for the first occurrence of `\r\n\r\n`.
Returns the index one past the last byte of the match (so the
caller can tell where the body would start), or `-1` if the
marker is not present. `stop = used - 3` ensures the 4-byte
lookahead never runs past the buffer.

    find_header_end(req: *byte, used: int) -> int
        if used < 4
            return -1
        val stop = used - 3
        var i = 0
        while i < stop
            if req[i] == byte(0x0d) && req[i+1] == byte(0x0a) && req[i+2] == byte(0x0d) && req[i+3] == byte(0x0a)
                return i + 4
            i += 1
        return -1


Main. Listen on :8080, accept one client, drain its headers, reply,
close both sides, exit. Each step prints a tagged log line so a
failing run is visible in the serial trace.

    main(argv: []string)
        val lfd = tcp_listen(8080)
        if lfd < 0
            puts("httpd: listen failed\n")
            exit_with(1)
            return
        puts("httpd: listening on :8080 fd=")
        print_udec(lfd)
        puts("\n")

        var peer_ip: [4]byte
        var peer_port: [2]byte
        val cfd = tcp_accept(lfd, &peer_ip[0], &peer_port[0])
        if cfd < 0
            puts("httpd: accept failed\n")
            tcp_close(lfd)
            exit_with(2)
            return
        puts("httpd: accepted cfd=")
        print_udec(cfd)
        puts(" from ")
        print_udec(int(peer_ip[0]) & 0xff)
        puts(".")
        print_udec(int(peer_ip[1]) & 0xff)
        puts(".")
        print_udec(int(peer_ip[2]) & 0xff)
        puts(".")
        print_udec(int(peer_ip[3]) & 0xff)
        val pp = ((int(peer_port[0]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(peer_port[1]) & 0xff)
        puts(":")
        print_udec(pp)
        puts("\n")

Read loop. The request is TCP-stream data: each `tcp_recv` returns
whatever chunk of bytes has arrived so far. We accumulate into
`rbuf` and re-scan after every recv. 1024 bytes holds the request
line plus any curl-style headers with huge room to spare.

        var rbuf: [1024]byte
        var used = 0
        var hdr_end = -1
        while used < 1024 && hdr_end < 0
            val got = tcp_recv(cfd, &rbuf[used], 1024 - used)
            if got < 0
                puts("httpd: recv failed\n")
                tcp_close(cfd)
                tcp_close(lfd)
                exit_with(3)
                return
            if got == 0
                break
            used += got
            hdr_end = find_header_end(&rbuf[0], used)
        if hdr_end < 0
            puts("httpd: bad request (no CRLFCRLF in ")
            print_udec(used)
            puts(" bytes)\n")
            tcp_close(cfd)
            tcp_close(lfd)
            exit_with(4)
            return
        puts("httpd: request hdr_end=")
        print_udec(hdr_end)
        puts("\n")

Canned response. 73 bytes — well under the 480-byte `tcp_send`
cap, so one call suffices. The `Content-Length: 6` matches
`hello\n` exactly so HTTP/1.0 clients that respect the length
(rare — most just read until close) get an accurate frame.

        val resp = "HTTP/1.0 200 OK\r\nContent-Type: text/plain\r\nContent-Length: 6\r\n\r\nhello\n"
        val rlen = len(resp)
        val rptr = *byte(resp)
        val sent = tcp_send(cfd, rptr, rlen)
        if sent < 0
            puts("httpd: send failed\n")
            tcp_close(cfd)
            tcp_close(lfd)
            exit_with(5)
            return
        puts("httpd: sent=")
        print_udec(sent)
        puts("\n")

        val cr = tcp_close(cfd)
        if cr < 0
            puts("httpd: close cfd failed\n")
            tcp_close(lfd)
            exit_with(6)
            return
        val lr = tcp_close(lfd)
        if lr < 0
            puts("httpd: close lfd failed\n")
            exit_with(7)
            return
        puts("httpd: done\n")
        exit()
